I actually found a work around to do this. I use shiny to create a file with all the inputs, and inotify  <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inotify>to monitor changes to that file. Once inotify detects a changes, it runs an rscript that runs some code and sends me an email when things are done.

On Mon, Mar 13, 2017 at 2:42 PM Andy Kipp <an...@rstudio.com> wrote:

> Applications running on shinyapps.io <http://shinyapps.io> will terminate 15 minutes after the last user of the application closes their browser. Your use-case doesn't sound like something that would work well on shinyapps.io <http://shinyapps.io>, as the platform is designed for interactive applications versus long running jobs.
>
> You might look into a platform like https://www.dominodatalab.com/
>
> On Thu, Mar 9, 2017 at 1:05 PM, Ignacio Martinez <igna...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Hi everyone,
> >
> > Suppose I have a function f(x,y,z) that takes a while to run, for example 48 hours. Is it possible to create a shiny app that allows me to choose x y z, and then runs this function on the background and sends me a notification with rpushbullet?
